 - Every commit message follows this repository's changelog contract. Use `<type>(<scope>)?: <subject>`, with type `feat`, `fix`, `perf`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, or `release`. The optional scope is lowercase. Write an imperative lowercase subject with no trailing period. About 72 characters is a readability target, not a validated limit or a reason to rewrite a commit. Reserve `feat`, `fix`, and `perf` for observable shipped-product outcomes, with subjects that stand alone as release notes. Use `chore` for CI, release-process, and repository-tooling work, including speed improvements. Add a `Changelog:` trailer when the engineering subject differs from the user-facing sentence. Pair `!` after the type with a `BREAKING CHANGE:` body paragraph stating what breaks and what the user must do. Group one complete user-visible unit per commit, including its supporting tests and refactors. Validate a draft message before committing by piping it to `bun run scripts/commit-contract.ts check-message`; `check --base <sha> --head <sha>` validates existing commits. Public release notes are generated from commit history: `bun run release` reads every commit whose header parses and drops the rest, so a header that does not parse is a release note lost.
 
+- There are other agents working on this tree. NEVER discard changes not related to your work! It is not because your subagent went rogue, it is because another independent agent working at my direction made changes and I do not want them lost! Never stash those changes, including through `--autostash`, and never remove them. Run the standard validation when it can produce a meaningful signal; if unrelated concurrent work blocks a global gate, run the narrowest relevant validation for your touched files. Do not "fix" any other agent's files unless asked. When a gate fails in another chat's file, tell that chat once (file, gate, exact error), then ignore failures in that file and publish your own work. Do not wait for its fix, coordinate its commits, or take over its publication. When the installed build fails on another chat's uncommitted work, build and verify from a worktree at your own commit. Knowing your own changes never requires git: track the files you touch yourself, and do not survey git status or diffs to protect concurrent work; simply avoid files outside your task. When publishing in a concurrent worktree, stage and commit each whole file your unit touched, including concurrent changes already present in that file. Never partial-stage, split, or omit another agent's changes within a touched file. Other agents can revise their work in later commits. Commits are the remote log of work completed, not rollback boundaries.
+- Keep local `main` at `origin/main`. Every committing agent brings in newer remote work itself; it is never a separate catch-up task. Before final verification, run `git merge --ff-only origin/main`. When Git refuses because of uncommitted overlap, continue: Ship integrates at publication by committing first and then merging, and names the selected files that the merge changed, which the caller checks again. After a push from a worktree, Ship also fast-forwards the local checkout of `main`. Never clone this repository on this machine: a clone's push leaves `main` and its tags behind origin. Use a worktree for a clean or isolated tree; `bun run release` fast-forwards `main` after its push.
 - Startup responsiveness is load-bearing. Keep first-frame rendering, prompt input, and chat runner readiness free of noncritical synchronous work and contention. Defer optional initialization, maintenance, preloading, and views through owned background or lazy execution with contained failures. A change that can affect startup must pass `bun run test:perf`; a measured startup regression greater than 20% is never acceptable.
